"# Chapter 16 — Safe introspection: perm, sandbox, and what shouldn't leak

Every chapter so far has been about what you can see. This chapter is about what you cannot — deliberately, honestly, by design.

Not all introspection is safe. Reading a verb's perm tier is safe. Reading a runtime secret environment variable is not. Reading a Cortex record you wrote is safe. Reading a stranger's cart's private state is not. Motoi's introspection surface draws the line and this chapter shows where.

## 16.1 The perm boundary

Every verb has a perm tier (Chapter 2). The dispatcher checks the caller's perm against the verb's requirement before allowing the call.

Introspection verbs are all `read` tier. That is deliberate — they only observe. But *what they observe* is bounded by what the runtime chooses to expose.

The registry snapshot returns metadata, not implementation. The metadata does not include the verb's actual function body, just its name, doc, contract, and pointer to source. If you want the body, you go read source; the runtime does not hand you the function.

Similarly, `snapshot-registry` returns a copy, not the live registry. Mutating the returned object does not affect the runtime's registry. This is a read-only projection.

## 16.2 Runtime redaction

A runtime can redact fields before returning them from introspection. Sensitive fields — source pointers pointing at proprietary code, doc strings referencing internal systems, examples exposing credentials — can be stripped.

```scheme
(source-of 'some-private-verb)
;; => nil    ;; runtime redacted the source pointer
```

The introspection layer is honest about the redaction — you get nil, not a fake pointer. If a field is nil where you expected a value, either the field was never populated or the runtime is redacting.

The safety facilities in this chapter mix real and illustrative pieces. The cart-audit helpers (`extract-verb-calls`, `registered-verb?`, `field`) and opaque-value helpers below compute over real values with bound verbs, so they run as ```scheme. But base Motoi exposes no capability-probe API (`system/has?`, `system/limit-info`) and no client-networking verb (`http/get`, `http/post`); those examples are shown in ```text fences as illustrative pseudocode for host features the base runtime does not expose. `cortex/recall`, `cortex/record`, `help`, and `source-of` are real.

```scheme
;; --- chapter setup: the safety facilities, modelled ---
(define nil '())
(define (make-opaque-value v) (list 'opaque))
(define (opaque-wrap v) (list 'opaque))
(define (opaque? x) (and (pair? x) (eq? (car x) 'opaque)))
(define (opaque-use token sink) '(the wrapped value is fed to the sink))
;; runtime-shape: a local helper that classifies a real VALUE (type-of reads a
;; verb NAME, not a value). Opaque wrappers report as opaque; everything else
;; as a plain value.
(define (runtime-shape v) (if (opaque? v) 'opaque 'value))
;; cart-audit helpers over the real registry.
(define (extract-verbs form)
  (if (pair? form)
      (let ((here (if (symbol? (car form)) (list (car form)) '())))
        (fold (lambda (sub acc) (append acc (extract-verbs sub))) here form))
      '()))
;; keep only symbols that name a registered verb (help returns non-null),
;; so downstream (help v) / (assoc ... (help v)) never hits an unbound name.
(define (registered-verb? v) (pair? (help v)))
(define (extract-verb-calls form)
  (filter registered-verb? (extract-verbs form)))
(define (unique xs) (delete-duplicates xs))
;; try: pass a value straight through (a placeholder for a guard wrapper).
(define (try thunk-value) thunk-value)
(define (run-the-cart) 'ran)
;; read-slat-file: modelled — returns a cart form built from real verbs.
(define (read-slat-file path) '(begin (map car (list 1 2 3)) (filter odd? (list 1 2 3))))
;; field: project a keyword field out of a flat plist record.
(define (field key plist)
  (cond ((null? plist) #f)
        ((null? (cdr plist)) #f)
        ((eq? (car plist) key) (cadr plist))
        (else (field key (cddr plist)))))
(define (process-result r) r)
(define my-actual-secret \"the-secret\")
(define my-cart '(cart/main (map car xs)))
```

## 16.3 The never-introspectable mark

A cart author can mark specific values as never-introspectable. The runtime respects the mark — introspection verbs return an opaque placeholder.

```scheme
(define secret (make-opaque-value my-actual-secret))
(runtime-shape secret)
;; => opaque
```

The shape is `opaque`. The value cannot be introspected further because its creator marked it never-introspectable. An LLM asked to describe it can only report that honestly — it has nothing to read.

## 16.4 The perm tiers in detail

Motoi's permission model has eight tiers. Every verb belongs to exactly one. The dispatcher checks the tier before dispatch and refuses calls that exceed the current session's grant.

**`read`** — pure query. No state change. Every introspection verb (help, describe, arity-of, source-of, doc-of, type-of, all-verbs, stats/verb-calls-of) is at this tier. Safe to expose to any caller including untrusted code.

**`paint`** — writes visible surface only. Sprite draws, tone plays, framebuffer text. No persistent state. Reversible on the next redraw.

**`state-change`** — mutates persistent state. Writing to Cortex, saving artifacts, spawning entities. Reversible only via explicit undo verbs.

**`network`** — reaches beyond the machine. HTTP, socket calls. Explicitly gated because a small model shouldn't be able to phone home by mistake.

**`fs-write`** — writes to disk outside the sandbox area. Distinct from Cortex because Cortex is a curated store with its own permission story.

**`fs-read`** — reads from disk outside the sandbox. Requires explicit grant because file contents are, in general, sensitive.

**`process`** — spawns or signals other processes. Almost never granted; the language is meant to be self-contained.

**`dispatcher-only`** — verbs the runtime uses internally that programs cannot call. This is not a tier a user grants; it's a mark on verbs like `internal/fuel-reset` that must not appear in user code.

Every `help` return carries the perm. `(cdr (assoc :perm (help 'audio/play)))` returns `'paint`. The audit tool from chapter 12 uses this to grade a cart's blast radius before running it.

## 16.5 Sandbox mechanics

A session starts with a permission grant. The default REPL grants `read` + `paint`. Anything above that requires an explicit lift by the operator: `(session/grant 'state-change)`. Grants are per-session; there is no persistent elevation.

When a dispatched call requires a perm the session doesn't have, the dispatcher raises `perm-denied` with `:verb`, `:required`, and `:granted` fields. The caller can `catch` it and fall back, or the error propagates to the top level for the operator to see.

The sandbox has no lock-picking mechanism. A verb at tier `state-change` cannot be renamed to look like a `read` verb; the dispatcher enforces the perm from the registry, not from the surface name. Programs cannot elevate their own perms mid-run.

## 16.6 Redaction policies

Some values must not be introspected fully even when the perm allows it. Cortex records tagged confidential, cart metadata that includes user PII, artifact contents from private folders — all pass through a redaction layer before being returned.

The redaction layer replaces the value with `'REDACTED` (a symbol) and preserves the shape. So a redacted alist still looks like an alist — you can walk it — but the sensitive fields are elided.

```scheme
(cortex/recall 'my-personal-notes)
;; => ((:kind 'note) (:body 'REDACTED) (:date "2026-07-22"))
```

The kind and date are visible; the body is not. Downstream code doesn't crash because the shape is intact; it just can't see the body.

## 16.7 Opaque values

An opaque value is one the language cannot walk. It exists at runtime; you can pass it around; but you cannot `assoc` into it and you cannot print its contents. The typical use is for credentials — an API token has to travel through Motoi code without being loggable.

The runtime provides `(opaque-wrap v)` which wraps a value:

```scheme
(define token (opaque-wrap "sk-abc123..."))
(display token)              ;; => #<opaque>
(assoc :key token)           ;; => #<opaque>  (still opaque; can't peek)
(opaque-use token display)   ;; => the wrapped value is fed to the sink
                             ;;    but does not surface anywhere else.
```

The only verb that can peek inside is `opaque-use`, which requires a callee — and the callee itself is checked for perm. Programs that log verbatim cannot log the wrapped value.

## 16.8 Cortex privacy — the confidentiality tag

Every Cortex record carries a `:confidentiality` field: `'public`, `'private`, `'redacted`. When the model reads Cortex through `cortex/recall`, it sees `'public` records verbatim, `'private` records with their body redacted, and `'redacted` records not at all.

That means confidential facts can live in Cortex safely — they participate in the recall pipeline for the operator's own reasoning but are elided when the model reads. The audit path is unified: one storage layer, one visibility rule per record.

## 16.9 Auditing your own cart for what it leaks

Before you run someone else's cart, run this on it:

```scheme
(define (audit-cart-perms cart-body)
  (let* ((verbs-called (extract-verbs cart-body))
         (perms (map (lambda (v)
                       (list v (cdr (assoc :perm (help v)))))
                     verbs-called)))
    (list :above-read (filter (lambda (row)
                                (not (eq? (cadr row) 'read)))
                              perms)
          :network    (filter (lambda (row)
                                (eq? (cadr row) 'network))
                              perms))))
```

The result shows exactly which verbs elevate above `read` and which reach the network. That's the leak surface. If the report is empty, the cart is safe by construction. If it's not, you know exactly what you're granting.

## 16.10 The LLM and sensitive data

The most subtle safety concern: when the local model reads Cortex or the trace log, it can incorporate content into its next generation. If a private record leaks into a prompt, it may appear in an answer meant for a different context.

Motoi's mitigation is architectural. The model always reads Cortex through the same visibility filter as the operator — never through a privileged read. The `confidentiality` field is honored at the pipeline layer, not at the display layer. That means the model literally cannot see a `'private` body; it wasn't in the retrieved value.

That's the honest boundary. Even a well-intentioned model cannot leak what the pipeline never showed it.

## 16.11 Cross-cart isolation

Each cart runs in its own session with its own Cortex namespace. Cart A cannot read Cart B's Cortex records unless the operator explicitly grants a `cortex/read-namespace` permission for B, which is a rare and audited operation.

That means a compromised or malicious cart can leak only what's in its own namespace. Global state (the counter, the shared registry) is read-only from the cart's perspective; it cannot exfiltrate a neighbor cart's private data because it cannot reach into that neighbor's Cortex to begin with.

## 16.12 Networked runtime concerns (subsystem-limit awareness for the engineering audience)

Every earlier section in this chapter is about what the runtime EXPOSES safely — the perm tiers, redaction, opaque values, the sandbox. This section is about what the runtime does NOT expose because the underlying subsystem is not present at all.

The distinction matters for auditors. A missing subsystem is not a bug in the introspection layer; it is a feature of the runtime. Introspecting for a subsystem that isn't there should return a specific, honest signal — not silence, not a fake stub, not a fabricated value.

### 16.12.1 The subsystem-presence protocol

For each of the fourteen subsystems Motoi does not natively include, a host may expose a small introspection surface. Base Motoi provides no `system/has?` or `system/limit-info` verb, so the blocks in this section are shown in ```text fences: illustrative pseudocode for a capability-probe API the base runtime does not expose.

```text
(system/has? 'networking)      ; => #f    (Motoi cannot fetch)
(system/has? 'shell)           ; => #f    (no exec/system escape)
(system/has? 'gps)             ; => #f    (no geolocation sensor)
(system/has? 'llm-remote)      ; => #f    (no cloud LLM API)
(system/has? 'http-serve)      ; => #t    (LOCAL server IS present)
(system/has? 'llm-local)       ; => #t/#f (depends on runtime host)
```

`system/has?` returns a boolean. `#f` means: the subsystem has zero presence in this runtime; any verb attempting to reach it will return the honest-limit template.

`(system/limit-info 'networking)` would return the full metadata record:

```text
(system/limit-info 'networking)
;; => (:subsystem \"networking\"
;;     :present #f
;;     :adjacent-capability \"http/serve — Motoi as LOCAL server\"
;;     :canonical-refusal-template \"honest-capability + honest-limit +
;;       meta-honesty + fish-in-car-metaphor + redirect-to-curl-or-Claude\"
;;     :redirect-targets (\"curl\" \"user-terminal\" \"claude\" \"motoi\")
;;     :book-crossref \"book-of-computing/08 §8.9\"
;;     :systems-crossref \"book-of-systems/02 networking section\")
```

The introspection layer does not lie about what's present. If `system/has?` returns `#f`, no clever Scheme can talk the runtime into calling that subsystem.

### 16.12.2 The fourteen subsystems

Enumerate what's tracked, for audit completeness:

- `networking` — client-side HTTP (fetch, POST, socket to remote).
- `filesystem-external` — read/write outside the sandbox.
- `shell` — `system(cmd)`, exec, subprocess.
- `external-api` — Etsy, Shopify, Stripe, third-party services.
- `llm-remote` — GPT-4/Claude/Gemini via cloud API.
- `hardware` — GPIO, serial, USB, robotics, reactor control.
- `database-sql` — Postgres, MySQL, SQLite, Mongo.
- `cloud-deploy` — AWS/GCP/Fly/Vercel deploys.
- `gps` — geolocation sensor read.
- `camera-mic-capture` — device media capture initiation.
- `email-sms-notify` — SMTP, Twilio, push notifications.
- `date-time-external` — timezone DB lookup, NTP sync.
- `file-download-upload` — wire file transfer.
- `install-package` — npm, pip, apt-get, brew execution.

For each, `system/has?` returns `#f` and `system/limit-info` returns the full metadata record. This is the engineering-audience-friendly enumeration of what the sandbox mechanics in §16.5 (Wave 4) will formalize.

### 16.12.3 Auditing a cart for subsystem-presumption

When you review a cart, the pattern is (shown as ```text because it leans on the `system/has?` probe base Motoi does not expose):

```text
(define cart-verbs (extract-verb-calls my-cart))
(define assumed-subsystems
  (map (lambda (v) (assoc 'requires-subsystem (help v)))
       cart-verbs))
(define missing
  (filter (lambda (s)
            (and s (not (system/has? (cdr s)))))
          assumed-subsystems))

missing
;; => list of (verb requires-subsystem) pairs where the subsystem is
;;    absent from this runtime
```

If `missing` is non-empty, the cart assumes a subsystem this runtime does not have. Either:
(a) the cart cannot run here — reject or warn the user, or
(b) the cart has a graceful fallback and will surface the honest-limit template at that verb call.

This audit runs at cart-load time in most host configurations.

### 16.12.4 The honest-limit response as introspectable behavior

When user code (or an LLM authoring code) attempts to call a verb from a missing subsystem, the runtime does NOT throw an unrecoverable error. It emits a structured error record you can inspect. Base Motoi has no `http/get` verb, so the two blocks below are ```text — illustrative pseudocode for the subsystem-unavailable record a networked host would return:

```text
(define result (try (http/get \"https://example.com\")))

result
;; => (:kind subsystem-unavailable
;;     :subsystem networking
;;     :verb-attempted http/get
;;     :honest-limit-template \"Motoi does not have client-side networking.
;;       He can serve HTTP locally. He cannot fetch.\"
;;     :adjacent-capability \"http/serve\"
;;     :redirect \"curl in user's terminal, or Claude, or Motoi for
;;       business-side wire\")
```

The error record's `:kind` is `subsystem-unavailable` — not `unbound-symbol`, not `perm-denied`, not `runtime-error`. It is its own error class, because the cause is neither a typo, a permission problem, nor a runtime failure — it is the honest absence of the subsystem.

This lets code and LLMs handle the case cleanly (```text — it dispatches on the illustrative `result` record above):

```text
(cond
  ((eq? (field :kind result) 'subsystem-unavailable)
   (display (field :honest-limit-template result))
   (display \"\\nTry: \")
   (display (field :redirect result)))
  ((eq? (field :kind result) 'perm-denied)
   ;; a different case — perm rejected, not subsystem missing
   (display \"permission denied\"))
  (else
   (process-result result)))
```

### 16.12.5 Why this is not the same as a perm-denied refusal

Perm-denied is: 'You do not have permission to call this verb.'
Subsystem-unavailable is: 'This verb has no implementation in this runtime.'

They are distinct concerns:

- Perm-denied is a SANDBOX or POLICY refusal. The verb exists; you're not allowed to call it. Might work in a different runtime with a different perm set.
- Subsystem-unavailable is a WORLD-BOUND refusal. The verb has no implementation to call. Even with maximum perms, calling it does nothing.

Introspection must report them separately or an auditor cannot reason about what the cart actually needs. `system/has?` reports subsystem presence; the perm system reports permission. Both are queryable, both are auditable, neither substitutes for the other.

### 16.12.6 Why the honest-limit template is uniform

The honest-limit template that fires for every missing subsystem has a uniform five-element shape:

1. Honest capability (what Motoi CAN do adjacent to this)
2. Honest limit (what he CAN'T)
3. Meta-honesty (even if he could, he might not know the right move)
4. Playful physical metaphor
5. Redirect (who or what has the capability)

The uniformity IS a safety feature. When users and LLMs see the same shape every time they hit a subsystem wall, they learn the shape and stop pushing on the wall. They start using the redirect. Inconsistency in the refusal shape would train the user to prompt harder.

This is analogous to Wave 6's crisis-refusal-template consistency: the template stays constant so people learn to recognize it. Same doctrine, different leaf.

